NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON TAYLOR V. STURGELL
1. Taylor v. Sturgell is a case whose outcome depends on the precise application of doctrine to a narrow set of facts. It is an exemplar of a case that carefully articulates and then applies long-standing doctrine to a peculiar—but not necessarily unusual—fact pattern. Also noteworthy about the case is its precise and plain-spoken treatment of conditions under which nonparties to a judgment are nonetheless bound by the judgment. In contrast to some of the 19th and 20th Century cases we’ve read so far, Justice Ginsburg’s opinion steers away from mystifying and ambiguous terms like “privity,” “estoppel,” and so on. The enumeration in Part II-B of the opinion of the six types of exceptions to the rule against nonparty preclusion may be the clearest statement of that doctrine you’ll find. Isn’t it refreshing when a court says exactly what it means?
